---
import type { OutlineEntry } from '../components/outline-entry';
import ResultsTable from '../components/ResultsTable.astro';
import { cssResults } from '../config/css-results';
import type { DebatePart } from '../config/debate-part';
import { debateParts } from '../config/debate-parts';
import PartLayout from '../layouts/PartLayout.astro';

const part: DebatePart | undefined = debateParts.find((candidate: DebatePart): boolean => candidate.path === '/CSS');

if (part === undefined) {
    throw new Error('src/config/debate-parts.ts has no part at /CSS.');
}

const outline: ReadonlyArray<OutlineEntry> = [
    { id: 'rules', text: 'Every rule is an if' },
    { id: 'choosing', text: 'Choosing' },
    { id: 'calculating', text: 'Calculating' },
    { id: 'limits', text: 'What CSS can\'t do alone, usually' },
    { id: 'beyond-html', text: 'Not just for HTML' },
    { id: 'score', text: 'How CSS scores' },
];

const positions: ReadonlyArray<number> = Array.from({ length: 24 }, (_: unknown, index: number): number => index + 1);

const ruleExample: string = `li:nth-child(3n) {
    background: coral;
}`;

const positionsExample: string = `li { counter-increment: position; }
li::before { content: counter(position); }

li:nth-child(3n) { /* every third: filled */ }
li:nth-child(4n) { /* every fourth: ringed */ }`;

const environmentExample: string = `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .scheme::before { content: "Your device is set to dark mode."; }
}

@container (width < 30rem) {
    .box::before { content: "This box is narrower than 30rem."; }
}`;

const arithmeticExample: string = `.times {
    counter-reset: result calc(var(--a) * var(--b));
}

.times::after {
    content: counter(result);
}`;

const emptyPageExample: string = `<!doctype html>
<style>
    html::before {
        content: "There is no body text on this page.";
    }
</style>`;

const xmlExample: string = `<?xml version="1.0"?>
<?xml-stylesheet type="text/css" href="recipe.css"?>
<recipe>
    <title>Toast</title>
    <step>Put bread in toaster.</step>
</recipe>`;
---
<PartLayout part={part} outline={outline}>
    <p>
        Most people who've written CSS think of it as the paint: colours, fonts, spacing, the bit that makes a page look
        right. That's what it was made for. But to do that job in a world of different screens, settings and states,
        CSS has grown a surprising amount of logic. This part looks at CSS on its own, setting aside what the HTML
        around it can do, and scores it against the four tests from <a href="/Programming">part 1</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        One thing to admit up front: CSS can't be looked at entirely alone, because it needs a document to apply to.
        HTML works without CSS; CSS doesn't work without something to style. We'll come back to that at the end, because
        the something doesn't have to be HTML.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rules">Every rule is an if</h2>
    <p>A CSS rule has two halves: a selector, and the declarations that follow it.</p>
    <pre><code>{ruleExample}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Read it as a condition: <em>if</em> an element is the third, sixth, ninth… child of its parent, <em>then</em> give
        it a coral background. The browser checks that condition against every element in the document, all the time,
        and applies the result. When several rules match the same element and disagree, the cascade decides which wins,
        by fixed rules of precedence. So a stylesheet isn't a list of colours. It's a set of conditions and outcomes that
        the browser keeps evaluating.
    </p>

    <h2 id="choosing">Choosing</h2>
    <p>
        Selectors are CSS's way of choosing. They can test an element's position (<code>:nth-child(3n)</code>), its
        type and attributes, its state (<code>:hover</code>, <code>:checked</code>, <code>:invalid</code>), and, since
        <code>:has()</code> reached every major browser in 2023, what it contains. They can be combined with "not"
        (<code>:not()</code>), "or" (<code>:is()</code>) and "and" (simply writing two together), which is everything you
        need to build any logical condition.
    </p>
    <p>
        Here, the HTML is nothing but twenty-four empty list items. CSS numbers them, fills every third and rings every
        fourth. The twelfth and twenty-fourth match both conditions, so they get both.
    </p>

    <div class="demo surface">
        <ol class="positions" role="list" aria-label="Twenty-four numbered squares">
            {positions.map(() => <li></li>)}
        </ol>
        <pre><code>{positionsExample}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        CSS can also choose based on its surroundings. Media queries test the device and the visitor's preferences;
        container queries test the size of the space an element sits in. Each is a branch: if this is true, use these
        rules; otherwise, use those. The two sentences below are written by CSS, after checking your settings and the
        width of the box.
    </p>

    <div class="demo surface">
        <div class="environment">
            <p class="scheme"></p>
            <div class="box"></div>
        </div>
        <pre><code>{environmentExample}</code></pre>
        <p class="demo-note">Resize the window, or switch your device between light and dark, and the sentences change.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="calculating">Calculating</h2>
    <p>
        CSS has been able to do sums since <code>calc()</code> arrived over a decade ago, so that a sidebar could be "a
        third of the width, less a gap". Since then it has gained <code>min()</code>, <code>max()</code> and
        <code>clamp()</code>; rounding and remainders with <code>round()</code>, <code>mod()</code> and
        <code>rem()</code>; and powers, roots and trigonometry with <code>pow()</code>, <code>sqrt()</code>,
        <code>sin()</code> and their relatives.
    </p>
    <p>
        Custom properties (often called CSS variables) give values names, like <code>--gap: 1rem</code>, so they can be
        reused and calculated with. And counters, made for numbering headings and list items, can hold the result of a
        calculation and print it on the page. Put those together and CSS will do arithmetic in front of you. Each answer
        below is worked out by the browser's CSS engine:
    </p>

    <div class="demo surface">
        <ul class="sums" role="list">
            <li class="times" style="--a: 7; --b: 6"></li>
            <li class="remainder" style="--a: 17; --b: 5"></li>
            <li class="power" style="--a: 2; --b: 10"></li>
            <li class="root" style="--a: 144"></li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>{arithmeticExample}</code></pre>
        <p class="demo-note">
            So, with respect to Ben Romy, CSS can work out 2 + 2. What it can't do is ask anyone which numbers to use:
            the 7 and the 6 are written into the page.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p>
        And CSS keeps gaining logic. Newer versions of the language add an <code>if()</code> function, for choosing a
        value inline, and <code>@function</code>, for defining reusable calculations of your own, and both have begun
        arriving in browsers. Each release moves CSS a little further from "style sheet" and a little closer to
        "language".
    </p>

    <h2 id="limits">What CSS can't do alone, usually</h2>
    <p>For all that, CSS as most people write it is missing two things that programs depend on.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Input.</strong> CSS can only read the document it styles and the environment it's shown in: the
            screen, the settings, where the pointer is. It can't be handed new information to work on.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Memory.</strong> A rule applies while its condition is true and stops the moment it isn't: move the
            pointer away and <code>:hover</code> is forgotten. CSS can't change the document to leave a note for itself,
            so in everyday use, nothing it works out is kept for a later step.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Without memory there's no way to repeat a step and build on the result, which is what computing anything
        requires. So for most of its life, CSS could decide and calculate, but only about a world it couldn't change.
    </p>
    <p>
        The exception is animation. An animation is CSS changing values over time by itself, with nothing else involved,
        and with enough ingenuity, and the newest features above, animations can act as a clock and carry values from one
        step to the next. In 2026 that was enough for someone to build a working processor in CSS, which
        <a href="/Together#limit">part 4</a> comes to. It's a long way from everyday CSS, and so far it only runs in some
        browsers, but it means "CSS has no memory" is no longer simply true.
    </p>

    <h2 id="beyond-html">Not just for HTML</h2>
    <p>
        CSS needs a document, but it isn't tied to HTML. Browsers apply CSS to SVG graphics, and to plain XML documents
        that point to a stylesheet, like this made-up recipe format:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{xmlExample}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Open that in a browser and <code>recipe.css</code> decides how a <code>&lt;recipe&gt;</code> and its steps are
        shown, though the browser knows nothing about recipes.
    </p>
    <p>
        You can even give CSS almost nothing to work with. A page whose only content is a stylesheet still has a
        document: the browser quietly adds the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and
        <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> elements it expects, and CSS can draw on them.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{emptyPageExample}</code></pre>
    <p>
        So the honest picture is this: CSS needs some document, but it treats that document as raw material, the way a
        program treats its input.
    </p>

    <h2 id="score">How CSS scores</h2>
    <p>Against the four tests, CSS on its own comes out like this:</p>

    <ResultsTable subject="CSS" results={cssResults} />

    <p>
        It's a closer result than most people expect, and a different shape from HTML's. HTML can hold state but can't
        make decisions about it; CSS can make decisions all day, but in everyday use has no state of its own to make them
        about. Each has exactly what the other is missing. <a href="/Together">Part 4</a> puts them together.
    </p>
</PartLayout>

<style>
    .demo {
        display: grid;
        gap: 1.25rem;
        padding: 1.5rem 1.75rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    }

    .demo > :global(*) {
        margin-top: 0;
    }

    .demo pre {
        background: var(--surface-alt);
    }

    .demo-note {
        color: var(--text-muted);
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
    }

    /* Choosing by position: numbered by a counter, filled every third, ringed every fourth. */
    .positions {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(2.75rem, 1fr));
        gap: 0.5rem;
        padding: 0;
        counter-reset: position;
    }

    .positions li {
        display: grid;
        place-items: center;
        aspect-ratio: 1;
        margin: 0;
        border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
        background: var(--surface-alt);
        color: var(--text-muted);
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
        counter-increment: position;
    }

    .positions li::before {
        content: counter(position);
    }

    .positions li:nth-child(3n) {
        background: var(--brand-coral);
        color: #000;
        font-weight: 600;
    }

    .positions li:nth-child(4n) {
        box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 3px var(--brand-blue);
        color: var(--heading);
        font-weight: 600;
    }

    .positions li:nth-child(3n):nth-child(4n) {
        color: #000;
    }

    /* Choosing by surroundings: the visitor's colour scheme, and the width of the box. */
    .environment {
        container-type: inline-size;
        display: grid;
        gap: 0.75rem;
    }

    .scheme,
    .box {
        padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        background: var(--surface-alt);
        color: var(--heading);
        font-weight: 500;
    }

    .scheme::before {
        content: 'Your device is set to light mode.';
    }

    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        .scheme::before {
            content: 'Your device is set to dark mode.';
        }
    }

    .box::before {
        content: 'This box is 30rem wide or more.';
    }

    @container (width < 30rem) {
        .box::before {
            content: 'This box is narrower than 30rem.';
        }
    }

    /* Calculating: each line's numbers come from its style attribute; the answer is worked out here. */
    .sums {
        display: grid;
        gap: 0.5rem;
        padding: 0;
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: var(--text-lg);
    }

    .sums li {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0.6rem 1rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        background: var(--surface-alt);
        color: var(--heading);
    }

    .times {
        counter-reset: a var(--a) b var(--b) result calc(var(--a) * var(--b));
    }

    .times::before {
        content: counter(a) ' × ' counter(b) ' = ' counter(result);
    }

    .remainder {
        counter-reset: a var(--a) b var(--b) result mod(var(--a), var(--b));
    }

    .remainder::before {
        content: 'The remainder of ' counter(a) ' ÷ ' counter(b) ' = ' counter(result);
    }

    .power {
        counter-reset: a var(--a) b var(--b) result pow(var(--a), var(--b));
    }

    .power::before {
        content: counter(a) ' to the power ' counter(b) ' = ' counter(result);
    }

    .root {
        counter-reset: a var(--a) result sqrt(var(--a));
    }

    .root::before {
        content: 'The square root of ' counter(a) ' = ' counter(result);
    }
</style>
